\begin{abstract}
Vision token pruning accelerates Vision Transformer inference with negligible accuracy loss, but whether this robustness transfers to Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models remains untested across architectures. We evaluate five VLA models (450M--7B parameters) spanning three connector families on closed-loop robotic manipulation and identify four response classes governed by effective vision-to-LLM pipeline complexity. $\pi_0$ (linear projection) is \emph{immune}: offline action loss improves 2.4\% at 90\% removal. X-VLA (linear projection + 12-layer encoder) is \emph{threshold-graded}: robust at $k \geq 0.9$ (97.6\% Success Rate), then drops to 56.2\% at $k{=}0.7$. OpenVLA (MLP projector) degrades \emph{smoothly}: 74.0\% $\to$ 37.4\% at 50\% removal. SmolVLA (pixel-shuffle) hits a \emph{catastrophic cliff}: 0\% at 5\% removal. A controlled comparison between $\pi_0$ and OpenVLA (both 256 SigLIP tokens, identical encoder lineage) yields a 63 percentage-point gap at $k{=}0.1$, isolating the connector as causal. The coefficient of variation (CV) of token importance predicts this spectrum continuously ($0.038 \to 0.151 \to 0.215 \to 0.314$) from a single forward pass. For catastrophic-cliff architectures, we propose Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR), which recovers 60\%+ SR at 75\% compression (75\% KV-cache reduction) where all deletion methods yield 0\%, with a flat M-curve ($M{=}16 \approx M{=}128$). CV diagnostic combined with VTR forms a Diagnose-then-Compress framework for safe VLA token reduction.
\end{abstract}

